  "country": "TH",
  "notes": "Hand-picked, because eBird Status & Trends marks most Thai birds as rare and the automatic cut gives only about 180 species. The list uses GBIF/eBird record counts (November 2026) as a ranking signal plus birding knowledge, and covers what a visitor on a normal 2 to 3 week trip meets: Bangkok parks and gardens, the Bang Poo and Pak Thale shore (winter waders), the central plains and rice fields, Khao Yai, Kaeng Krachan, Doi Inthanon and the north, and the Krabi mangroves and southern peninsula, plus the common urban and farmland birds. Left out as rare, very local or needing special trips: vagrants, seabirds and boat trips, owls and nightjars other than the commonest, most skulkers, most leaf warblers and bush warblers, and the pheasants and partridges of deep forest. Thailand has 4 endemic-status species in the database; only the Rufous Limestone Babbler is in the trip (Wat Phra Phutthabat Noi and the Saraburi limestone). Gurney's Pitta (guides report it may be extirpated in Thailand), White-eyed River Martin (not seen for decades) and Turquoise-throated Barbet (a split of Blue-throated Barbet from the far south, rarely separated in the field) are dropped. Left out for lack of usable photos in the library or because the photos could not be identified with confidence: Collared Scops Owl (cosowl1), Golden-bellied Gerygone (gobger1), Pacific Swallow (pacswa1), Red-eyed Bulbul (reebul1), Rufous-fronted Babbler (rufbab10), Little Heron (strher1), Wreathed Hornbill (wrehor1), Asian Tit (cintit13), Collared Owlet (colowl1), Asian Dowitcher (asidow1) and Nordmann's Greenshank (norgre1), and Red Junglefowl (redjun), whose only photos in the library are feral roadside fowl.",
